 ## tab-keys.mjs
 
-### §stable-tab-keys — closing a tab never renumbers the others
+### §stable-tab-keys — closing a tab never renumbers the others (superseded 2026-10-23 by §contiguous-tab-keys)
 - Since: 2026-09-02
+- Status: superseded on 2026-10-23 — the maintainer chose contiguous renumbering on close; see §contiguous-tab-keys. The record below is kept as history.
 - Decision: a key is assigned once from `next` and is never reused or shifted. Closing a tab leaves a gap (`t1,t3`).
 - Forbidden: renumbering the remaining keys after a close to make them contiguous.
 - Reason: the key is the React key and the URL state. Shifting keys remounts every tab to the right of the closed one
   and drops its scroll position and unsaved form input (incident 2026-08-29, reproduced by the "close keeps keys" test).
 - Evidence: `tab-keys.test.mjs`
+
+### §contiguous-tab-keys — closing a tab renumbers the remaining tabs t1..tn
+- Since: 2026-10-23
+- Decision: `closeTab` renumbers the remaining tabs, in their current order, as `t1, t2, t3, …` and resets `next` to the
+  tab count + 1, so keys are always contiguous and the next `openTab` gets the next number. Closing `t2` of `t1,t2,t3`
+  gives `t1,t2`.
+- Forbidden: closing a tab without renumbering, or leaving `next` past the last key (the next open would skip a number).
+- Supersedes: §stable-tab-keys. The maintainer chose this on 2026-10-23.
+- Consequence: wherever `tab.key` is used as the React key, tabs to the right of a closed tab now remount (the cost
+  recorded in §stable-tab-keys; not re-measured). Any state keyed by a tab key (active tab, scroll, drafts, caches) must be
+  remapped in the same step as the close; old `?tabs=` URLs only match while no tab has been closed.
+- Evidence: `tab-keys.test.mjs`
